 # Chunked Voxel Mesh Generation with Face Culling and Quad Expansion
 
-Also called: Chunk-based Greedy Meshing, Greedy Meshing with Chunked Processing
+Also called: Chunk-based Greedy Meshing, FrontGreedyMesh, Greedy Meshing with Chunked Processing, TopGreedyMesh
 
 ## Overview
-Chunked Voxel Mesh Generation with Face Culling and Quad Expansion is a surface extraction algorithm that processes a 3D voxel chunk to generate a simplified triangle mesh by merging adjacent coplanar faces of identical voxel types into larger quads [S1, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of high vertex and draw call counts in naive voxel rendering by reducing the primitive count significantly compared to one-quad-per-face approaches [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings by explicitly handling neighbor chunk references to determine visibility across chunk boundaries and using a packed integer format for vertex attributes to minimize memory bandwidth [S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Binary Greedy Meshing with Packed Quads](3312-binary-greedy-meshing-with-packed-quads.md) which focuses on binary occupancy, this method supports multiple voxel types and ambient occlusion [S3, unofficial].
+Chunked Voxel Mesh Generation with Face Culling and Quad Expansion is a CPU-based algorithm that converts discrete 3D voxel data into optimized triangle meshes by merging adjacent coplanar faces of identical voxel types into larger quads [S1, S2, S4, S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of excessive vertex and draw call counts in voxel worlds by reducing primitive density while preserving sharp, axis-aligned surfaces suitable for architectural geometry [S6, unofficial]. It differs from standard face culling (which only removes hidden faces) by actively merging contiguous blocks into maximal axis-aligned rectangles, and it differs from Marching Cubes by maintaining discrete voxel boundaries rather than smoothing surfaces [S2, S6, unofficial]. This method builds on [Greedy Meshing](3519-greedy-meshing.md) by processing data in fixed-size chunks and handling inter-chunk visibility.
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: A `Chunk` object containing a 1D array of voxel data (`data`), neighbor chunk pointers (`cXN`, `cXP`, `cYN`, `cYP`, `cZN`, `cZP`), chunk world position (`chunkPosX`, `chunkPosY`, `chunkPosZ`), and heightmaps (`minAltitude`, `maxAltitude`) [S1, S4, unofficial].
-- Output: A vertex buffer (`vertexBuffer`) containing packed vertex attributes and an index buffer (or triangle list) defining the mesh geometry [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** A 1D array of voxel data representing a chunk (e.g., `blockArray1D`), neighbor chunk data for boundary checks, and metadata (chunk size, block size, world offset) [S1, S5, S6, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A mesh structure containing vertices, indices, and optionally UVs/normals [S2, S6, unofficial].
 
